1
00:00:00,393 --> 00:00:02,218
This time you’re the reviewer.

2
00:00:02,293 --> 00:00:06,993
In the approved remote terminal, confirm
that GitHub CLI is using your own

3
00:00:07,068 --> 00:00:10,283
authorized account and that you are in
the training repository.

4
00:00:10,875 --> 00:00:13,600
The author sends you pull request forty-
two.

5
00:00:13,675 --> 00:00:16,150
Open that request for this exercise.

6
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,940
We’ll check the intent first, then the
actual patch,

7
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:21,618
and finally the checks for the revision
you’re reviewing.

8
00:00:23,126 --> 00:00:27,151
Run the GitHub CLI view command for pull
request forty-two.

9
00:00:27,226 --> 00:00:29,276
Read the title and the issue link.

10
00:00:29,326 --> 00:00:33,588
The author says zero should be rejected
and all four unit tests now pass.

11
00:00:34,222 --> 00:00:36,247
That tells you what to look for.

12
00:00:36,322 --> 00:00:39,922
Keep the issue open beside the terminal
so you can compare the requested

13
00:00:39,972 --> 00:00:42,704
behavior with the change you’re about to
inspect.

14
00:00:44,231 --> 00:00:45,956
Now view the actual diff.

15
00:00:46,006 --> 00:00:48,931
The main change removes equality at the
boundary.

16
00:00:48,981 --> 00:00:52,304
The second change adds a test that calls
the validator with zero.

17
00:00:52,907 --> 00:00:56,357
Ask yourself whether that test would
fail on the old code.

18
00:00:56,432 --> 00:01:00,507
This is the difference between seeing a
test name and seeing a test that proves

19
00:01:00,557 --> 00:01:01,709
the relevant behavior.

20
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:05,910
Look up the pull request’s required
checks.

21
00:01:05,985 --> 00:01:09,310
In the training example, the unit test
check is required.

22
00:01:09,385 --> 00:01:12,584
Open its run if you need to confirm
which suite it executed.

23
00:01:13,208 --> 00:01:16,133
If the checks are pending, wait for the
result.

24
00:01:16,208 --> 00:01:19,973
If the author pushes again, refresh the
diff and checks for the new head before

25
00:01:20,023 --> 00:01:21,097
making your decision.

26
00:01:22,581 --> 00:01:24,656
Here’s the alternate outcome.

27
00:01:24,706 --> 00:01:29,356
The checks are green, but the test added
in this version exercises one and never

28
00:01:29,406 --> 00:01:31,020
calls the validator with zero.

29
00:01:31,623 --> 00:01:33,698
That leaves the bug untested.

30
00:01:33,773 --> 00:01:37,523
Use the request-changes option and ask
for the missing assertion.

31
00:01:37,598 --> 00:01:40,148
Give the author a specific behavior to
verify.

32
00:01:40,813 --> 00:01:43,300
Review the new revision after they
address it.

33
00:01:44,726 --> 00:01:49,076
Return to the version with the right
condition and the actual zero test.

34
00:01:49,151 --> 00:01:51,776
If you’ve checked the diff and the
required results,

35
00:01:51,826 --> 00:01:54,616
you can approve with a short statement
of what you verified.

36
00:01:55,275 --> 00:01:59,025
Submit the review from your account,
then read back the reviews on the pull

37
00:01:59,075 --> 00:02:00,200
request.

38
00:02:00,275 --> 00:02:03,390
Confirm your decision was saved to the
intended request.

39
00:02:04,851 --> 00:02:07,551
Pair up and swap author and reviewer
roles.

40
00:02:07,601 --> 00:02:11,626
Give each reviewer an issue, a pull
request, and the check results.

41
00:02:11,701 --> 00:02:13,753
In one run, the evidence is complete.

42
00:02:14,371 --> 00:02:17,336
In the other, leave out the test for the
key input.

43
00:02:17,411 --> 00:02:20,961
Ask the reviewer to explain their
decision in one sentence.

44
00:02:21,036 --> 00:02:24,326
It should tell the author what was
verified or what needs to change.
